herolte: deep idle wakes on the virtual timer; a core that does not come back is diagnosed with CPU_ON (0439)

min68 (heroidle=128): cpu 7 entered once (enter 1, resume 0); 15 wake-up SGIs and 3.2 s later the watchdog switched deep idle off; the system itself went on for 240 s. 0439: (1) the architected virtual timer is armed 2 ms ahead before CPU_SUSPEND, since an SGI may not wake a powered-down core, and the timer resynchronized if the call returns; (2) when the watchdog gives up, PSCI CPU_ON on that core: 0 means it was OFF (and it should come back through resume_idle_cpu), -4 ALREADY_ON or -5 ON_PENDING that it is still ON somewhere: HEROLTE IDLE line with the result. Still heroidle only (D-0089).

--- a/osfmk/arm64/cpu.c
+++ b/osfmk/arm64/cpu.c
@@ -503,6 +503,14 @@
 	herolte_idle_enter[c]++;
 	herolte_idle_since[c] = mach_absolute_time();
 	CleanPoC_Dcache();
+	/*
+	 * 0439: min68 (cpu 7 only): the core entered, never came back, 15 wake-up SGIs did nothing. An SGI may not be a wake source
+	 * of a powered-down core; the architected virtual timer (PPI 27, the one XNU uses) is what Linux's idle wakes on. Armed for
+	 * 2 ms ahead (52 000 ticks at 26 MHz) before the call; the timer is re-synchronized if the call returns.
+	 */
+	__builtin_arm_wsr64("CNTV_CVAL_EL0", __builtin_arm_rsr64("CNTVCT_EL0") + 52000ULL);
+	__builtin_arm_wsr64("CNTV_CTL_EL0", 1ULL);
+	__builtin_arm_isb(ISB_SY);
 	__builtin_arm_dsb(DSB_SY);
 	register uint64_t x0 __asm__("x0") = 0xC4000001ULL;     /* CPU_SUSPEND (the DTB's cpu_suspend) */
 	register uint64_t x1 __asm__("x1") = 0x10000ULL;        /* power state: power down (cpu-sleep) */
@@ -512,6 +520,7 @@
 	    "x4", "x5", "x6", "x7", "x8", "x9", "x10", "x11", "x12", "x13", "x14", "x15", "x16", "x17", "cc", "memory");
 	/* Only reached when the call returns: an error, or the core did not power down. */
 	herolte_idle_since[c] = 0;
+	timer_resync_deadlines();
 	return true;
 }
 #endif /* ARM64_BOARD_CONFIG_HEROLTE */
